WEEK 2 – JAVASCRIPT

During Week 2, I studied the fundamentals of JavaScript and how it is used to make web pages interactive and dynamic. JavaScript can be added to HTML using the <script> tag. It can be written directly inside an HTML file (internal JavaScript) or placed in a separate .js file and linked using <script src='script.js'></script> (external JavaScript). External JavaScript helps keep HTML and JavaScript code organized and easier to maintain.

I learned about variables, which are named containers used to store data. JavaScript uses 'let', 'const', and 'var' to declare variables. 'let' is used when a value may change, while 'const' is used when a variable should not be reassigned. 'var' is the older way of declaring variables. Example: 'let age = 20;' and 'const name = 'John';'. Variable names can contain letters, numbers, '_', and '$', but they cannot start with a number, contain spaces, or use reserved JavaScript keywords. JavaScript variable names are also case-sensitive.

I studied JavaScript data types, which describe the type of value stored in a variable. The main data types are String, Number, BigInt, Boolean, Null, Undefined, Object, and Symbol. A String represents text, for example 'let name = 'John';'. A Number represents integers and decimal numbers, for example 'let age = 20;' and 'let price = 25.5;'. BigInt is used for very large integers and is written with 'n' at the end, for example 'let bigNumber = 12345678901234567890n;'. Boolean has two values, 'true' and 'false'. 'null' represents an intentionally empty value, while 'undefined' means a variable has been declared but has not yet been assigned a value. Objects store related data using key-value pairs, while Symbols provide unique identifiers.

I learned about the 'typeof' operator, which is used to determine the data type of a value. For example, 'typeof 'Hello'' returns 'string', 'typeof 20' returns 'number', and 'typeof true' returns 'boolean'.

I also studied user interaction in JavaScript. The 'alert()' function displays a message to the user, for example 'alert('Hello!');'. The 'prompt()' function asks the user to enter information, for example 'let age = prompt('How old are you?');'. The value returned by 'prompt()' is normally a string. The 'confirm()' function displays a message with OK and Cancel buttons and returns either 'true' or 'false', for example 'let isBoss = confirm('Are you the boss?');'.

I learned about type conversion, which means changing a value from one data type to another. 'String()' converts a value to a string, 'Number()' converts a value to a number, and 'Boolean()' converts a value to a Boolean. For example, 'Number('25')' converts the string '25' into the number 25, while 'String(true)' converts 'true' into 'true'.

I studied arrays, which are used to store multiple values in one variable. An array is created using square brackets, for example 'let fruits = ['Apple', 'Orange', 'Pear'];'. Array indexes start at 0, so 'fruits[0]' is 'Apple', 'fruits[1]' is 'Orange', and 'fruits[2]' is 'Pear'. The 'length' property gives the number of elements in an array. Array elements can also be changed by assigning a new value to their index, for example 'fruits[2] = 'Lemon';'.

I learned several array methods. 'push()' adds an element to the end of an array, while 'pop()' removes the last element. 'shift()' removes the first element, while 'unshift()' adds an element to the beginning. 'splice()' can be used to add, remove, or replace elements in an array. For example, 'arr.splice(1, 1);' removes one element starting at index 1. 'slice()' creates a new array containing selected elements from another array without changing the original array. I also learned that arrays can be traversed using loops such as 'for' and 'for...of'.

I studied higher-level array methods such as 'filter()', 'map()', and 'reduce()'. 'filter()' creates a new array containing elements that satisfy a condition. For example, 'let result = users.filter(user => user.id < 3);'. 'map()' creates a new array by applying a function to every element, for example 'let names = users.map(user => user.name);'. 'reduce()' is used to combine all elements of an array into a single value, such as calculating a total: 'let result = arr.reduce((sum, current) => sum + current, 0);'.

I also studied objects. An object is a collection of related data stored using key-value pairs. For example, 'let user = { name: 'John', age: 30 };'. The properties of an object can be accessed using dot notation, such as 'user.name', or square brackets, such as 'user['name']'. Object properties can be changed, added, or deleted. For example, 'user.name = 'Peter';' changes a property, 'user.email = 'example@gmail.com';' adds a new property, and 'delete user.age;' removes a property.

I learned that objects are stored and handled using references. When one object variable is assigned to another, both variables can refer to the same object. For example, if 'let admin = user;' is used, changing 'admin.name' can also change 'user.name' because both variables refer to the same object.

I studied object methods. A method is a function stored as a property of an object. For example, 'let user = { name: 'John', sayHi() { alert('Hello!'); } };'. The method can then be called using 'user.sayHi();'.

I also learned about destructuring assignment. Destructuring allows values to be extracted from arrays or properties from objects and assigned to variables. For example, 'let arr = ['John', 'Smith']; let [firstName, surname] = arr;'. Object destructuring can be done using 'let {name, age} = user;'. I also studied the rest operator '...', which can collect the remaining values. For example, 'let [firstName, surname, ...rest] = arr;'. Rest parameters can also be used in functions to allow a function to accept any number of arguments, for example 'function showName(firstName, lastName, ...titles) { }'.

Finally, I studied variable scope. Scope determines where a variable can be accessed. Variables declared using 'let' and 'const' have block scope, meaning they are available within the block where they are declared. Variables declared inside a function belong to that function's scope. Variables declared outside functions or blocks can be accessed from wider or global scope depending on how they are declared.

Overall, Week 2 helped me understand the basic structure of JavaScript, variables, data types, user interaction, type conversion, arrays, array methods, objects, object methods, destructuring, rest parameters, and variable scope.